  .page-banner { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a365d 0%, #2c5282 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem 2rem; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 0; }
  .page-banner h1 { font-family: 'Cinzel', serif; font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: white; }
</style>
  <div class="lh-container">

<div class="page-wrapper">

  <h1 class="main-title">Likutay Halachos</h1>
  <h2 class="halacha-title">Yoreh Dayah &mdash; Laws of <em>Milah</em> 5 <span style="font-weight:normal;">(continued)</span></h2>

  <div class="subtitle-verse">
    Continuing from the preceding, which was based upon the Torah-teaching <em>&ldquo;And Boaz said to Ruth&rdquo;</em> etc.,
    in <em>Likutay Moharan</em> Vol.&nbsp;I, no.&nbsp;65 etc. &mdash; study it there thoroughly.
  </div>
  <div class="subtitle-sa">
    This section bridges to the laws of <em>Shulchan Aruch</em> Yoreh Dayah, <em>Hilchos Milah</em>, Siman&nbsp;5, Si'eef&nbsp;9
    (<em>Milas Avadim</em> &mdash; Circumcision of Slaves)
  </div>

  <div class="continuation-note">
    &#x2193; The main body of <em>Likutay Halachos</em> Milah&nbsp;5 concluded at section&nbsp;8 (&sect;&nbsp;ח), which treated the ancient <em>chassidim</em> who waited one hour before and one hour after prayer. The author now turns the same thematic framework &mdash; <em>bitul</em>, <em>reshimu</em>, rectification of vessels, drawing down the sealed <em>chassadim</em> &mdash; to illuminate the halachos of circumcision of slaves, a bridging technique characteristic of <em>Likutay Halachos</em>.
  </div>

  <span class="topic-badge">&#x2728; Topic: <em>Milas Avadim</em> &mdash; Circumcision of Slaves</span>

  <!-- SECTION 9 -->
  <span class="section-number">9</span>
  <p class="no-indent">
    And this is the aspect of the <em>mitzvah</em> of
    <span class="key-term">milas avadim</span> [circumcision of slaves] &mdash; for
    the slave is his [master's] monetary acquisition [<span class="key-term">kinyan kaspo</span>],
    and therefore [the master] is obligated to circumcise him, to remove the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span> from his monetary acquisition. For the
    essential purpose of the <em>mitzvah</em> of
    <span class="key-term">milah</span> &mdash; to remove the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span> from the holy flesh &mdash; is in order to
    remove and nullify the grip of the
    <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span> [the venom of the serpent] from
    the body, so that the body should be rectified and become a prepared and ready
    vessel to receive the light of the radiance [<span class="aramaic">or ha-zrichu</span>]
    of the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> [imprint/trace] from the
    <span class="key-term">bitul</span> [self-nullification] mentioned above. For the
    entire creation of the human being was
    <span class="aramaic">b'gin d'yishtamod'in lay</span> &mdash; [in Aramaic:] in
    order that they should know and recognize Him, blessed-be-He. And the primary
    knowledge of Him, blessed-be-He, is drawn through the
    <span class="key-term">bitul</span> mentioned above in the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">ratzo va-shov</span> [running and returning], through
    which, by means of the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above,
    light shines into one's <span class="key-term">da'as</span> [knowledge/mind]
    to know that Hashem is G-d and all is entirely good etc., as above; and as is
    explained at the conclusion of the Torah-teaching mentioned above regarding the
    saying of our Sages of blessed memory: that in the future the Holy Blessed One
    will make a circular dance [<em>machol</em>] for the righteous in the Time to Come,
    and each one will point with his finger and declare,
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;This is Hashem for Whom we have hoped&rdquo;</span>
    etc. &mdash; and from there all the Torah flows, as above.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the essential sum of all the Torah and <em>mitzvos</em> is to sanctify the
    body so that it will be a prepared vessel to receive the light of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above. For when the body is blemished
    through any transgression, G-d protect us, it then cannot nullify itself properly;
    and even if at times it prevails more and does nullify itself, it is only for a very
    brief instant; and moreover, after returning from the
    <span class="key-term">bitul</span>, the body is unable to receive the light of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> on account of its blemish &mdash; and then the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> passes and departs from him immediately, and
    he forgets Him, blessed-be-He, G-d protect us. Therefore Hashem blessed-be-He
    commanded us all the <em>mitzvos</em> of the Torah, in order to sanctify our bodies
    through them, so that we can receive the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span>
    mentioned above and bind it within our bodies well and always remember Him,
    blessed-be-He.
  </p>
  <p>
    And therefore, in truth, the complete perfection of attaining knowledge of His
    blessed Divinity is merited <span class="daikah">precisely</span> [<em>dayka</em>]
    by the great <span class="key-term">tzadikim</span> who have sanctified themselves
    properly, and have sealed and closed their eyes completely from the
    <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span> [the illusory sight of this world],
    and who are pure and holy and separated and removed from all the desires of this
    world &mdash; even those that are permitted &mdash; to the ultimate extreme; and
    only they merit attainments of Divinity through the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of the <span class="key-term">bitul</span>
    mentioned above, because their bodies are exceedingly, exceedingly pure, enabling
    them to receive the light of the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> in fullness
    and perfection, as above. But the Blessed Name, blessed-be-He, had compassion upon
    us and gave us the Torah through Moses &mdash; through which every single person can
    remember Him, blessed-be-He, by fulfilling the <em>mitzvos</em> of the Torah at the
    very least, through which his body is sanctified and he can receive some sparkling
    [<em>hitnoztzus</em>] from the radiance of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above.
    <span class="double-paren">((And see regarding this elsewhere.))</span>
    [The sparkling received is] according to his [spiritual] level, according to his
    fulfillment of the Torah.
  </p>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of the <em>mitzvah</em> of
    <span class="key-term">milah</span>, which is the foremost
    <em>mitzvah</em> [<em>mitzvah rishonah</em>], which is to remove the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span>, which is the
    <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span>, so that one can bind within one's
    body the light of the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above. And
    this is the aspect of the <em>mitzvah</em> of
    <span class="key-term">milas avadim</span> &mdash; those who are his monetary
    acquisition [<em>kinyan kaspo</em>] &mdash; in order to remove the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span> of the
    <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span> that grips in the
    <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span>, from his monetary acquisition.
    For the primary grip of the <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span>, which
    is the <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span>, takes hold most intensely in
    <span class="highlight-concept"><span class="key-term">mammon</span></span>
    [money/wealth] &mdash; for
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;silver answers everything&rdquo;</span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Ecclesiastes 10:19]</span> &mdash; and within it
    are encompassed all the vanities and desires of the follies of this world. For
    within <span class="key-term">mammon</span>, all thirty-nine
    <span class="key-term">melachos</span> [creative labors] have their grip &mdash;
    those which were drawn forth from the sin of Adam the first man through the counsel
    of the serpent. And therefore there is where the primary
    <span class="key-term">asiyah</span> [doing/making] and
    <span class="key-term">avdus</span> [servitude/labor] reside, being the sum of
    all thirty-nine <span class="key-term">melachos</span> &mdash; which are the
    precise opposite of the <span class="key-term">bitul</span> mentioned above.
    For the <span class="key-term">bitul</span> means to nullify the body completely
    so that it has no sensation whatsoever &mdash; even of the most necessary [bodily
    functions] &mdash; to the point where one would not move one's eyelids, would not
    move one's lips, would not stir any limb at all, as cited [in the traditions of
    the <em>tzadikim</em>]. Whereas the
    <span class="key-term">asiyah</span> and <span class="key-term">avdus</span> of all
    the thirty-nine <span class="key-term">melachos</span> &mdash; all of which are
    [driven by] the desire for <span class="key-term">mammon</span> &mdash; are the
    precise opposite of <span class="key-term">bitul</span> in every respect.
  </p>
  <p>
    And therefore one must sanctify all the more the thirty-nine
    <span class="key-term">melachos</span>, so that they should be in the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">meleches ha-mishkan</span> [the sacred labor of the
    Sanctuary] <span class="double-paren">((as explained elsewhere))</span> &mdash;
    in order to remove from them the <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span>
    that has gripped them most intensely, so that it should not prevent him from binding
    the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above within his heart when he
    returns from the <span class="key-term">bitul</span>; for then, upon returning
    from the <span class="key-term">bitul</span>, the aspect of
    <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span> overpowers anew with even greater
    force, in the manner of two people battling one another etc. And as is explained
    there regarding the intensification of <span class="key-term">yisoorim</span>
    [sufferings], G-d protect us &mdash; see it there. For it is all one: for the
    <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span> &mdash; which are the vanities and
    desires of this world &mdash; are themselves the primary source of suffering
    [<span class="key-term">yisoorim</span>] of the soul, and from them come all the
    bodily <span class="key-term">yisoorim</span> as well, G-d protect us.
  </p>
  <p>
    Therefore, one must circumcise the slave who is his monetary acquisition
    [<em>kinyan kaspo</em>], in order to remove the <span class="key-term">orlah</span>
    of the <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span> from the slave who is his
    monetary acquisition, wherein is the primary grip [of the
    <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span>], as above &mdash; so that [the
    slave] can receive the light of the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> and bind
    it within his heart when he returns from the <span class="key-term">bitul</span>,
    so that the <span class="key-term">orlah</span> and the
    <span class="aramaic">zuhama</span> [filth] of his slave &mdash; his monetary
    acquisition &mdash; should not impede him. For the primary grip of the
    <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span> &mdash; the
    <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span> &mdash; is in
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span>, wherein is the primary
    <span class="key-term">avdus</span>, as above. And therefore the slave is
    obligated in numerous <em>mitzvos</em>, and every <em>mitzvah</em> that a woman
    is obligated in, a slave is also obligated in &mdash; for one must sanctify the
    slave who is his monetary acquisition through the <em>mitzvos</em> of the Torah
    &mdash; and [this is] for [the slave's] own benefit &mdash; and to circumcise him
    specifically for the purpose of [his] enslavement [<em>l'shem avdus</em>], in
    order to nullify the <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span> from his
    monetary acquisition, wherein is the primary grip, as above.
  </p>

  <!-- SECTION 10 -->
  <span class="section-number">10</span>
  <p class="no-indent">
    And therefore, when the slave of a <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> [priest] is
    circumcised, he is permitted to eat of the
    <span class="key-term">terumah</span> [priestly tithe-portion] and of the
    <em>chazeh va-shok</em> [chest and thigh portions of offerings] &mdash; something
    that a [non-priestly] Israelite himself is forbidden [to eat]. For Hashem
    blessed-be-He sanctified us through His <em>mitzvos</em> so that we could recognize
    Him through the radiance of the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned
    above &mdash; and for this reason we must sanctify our eating, not eating forbidden
    things: <em>nevailos</em> [improperly slaughtered animals] and
    <em>treifos</em> [forbidden foods] and impure species, in which the
    <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span> has its greatest grip; for through
    consuming them the body is blemished and cannot receive the light of the radiance
    mentioned above.
  </p>
  <p>
    And just as impure things are forbidden for this reason, so conversely He also
    forbade for us numerous holy things that are forbidden to eat on account of the
    excess of their holiness &mdash; such as <span class="key-term">terumah</span> and
    sacrificial <em>kodashim</em> to a
    <span class="key-term">zar</span> [non-priest] &mdash; the reason for their
    prohibition being that [the <em>zar</em>] should not receive the vitality drawn
    through eating in a manner of excess light that would cause the
    <span class="key-term">sh'viras kelim</span> [shattering of the vessels], G-d
    protect us. For the primary drawing of the light mentioned above is through the
    rectification of the vessels, as above. Therefore the
    <span class="key-term">zar</span> is forbidden to eat
    <span class="key-term">terumah</span> and <em>kodashim</em> on account of excess
    light, for one who is not a <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> lacks the capacity
    to draw and bind the light that flows through eating
    <span class="key-term">terumah</span> and <em>kodashim</em>, for it constitutes
    excess light as far as he is concerned. But the slave &mdash; who is the monetary
    acquisition [<em>kinyan kaspo</em>] of the
    <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> &mdash; can eat
    <span class="key-term">terumah</span> and <em>kodashim</em> like his master the
    <span class="key-term">Kohen</span>, for he is included within him [the
    <em>Kohen</em>], since he is the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span>'s monetary
    acquisition [<em>kaspo</em>] &mdash; where is the primary completion of the
    rectification of the vessels. For the primary rectification of the vessels is
    accomplished by sanctifying <span class="key-term">mammon</span> from the grip of
    the <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span> that has gripped [it] through
    the thirty-nine <span class="key-term">melachos</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the <span class="key-term">Kohanim</span>'s service is in the Temple
    [<em>Beis ha-Mikdash</em>] and in the Sanctuary [<em>Mishkan</em>] &mdash; where
    is the primary rectification of all thirty-nine
    <span class="key-term">melachos</span>, as is explained elsewhere. And therefore
    they [the <span class="key-term">Kohanim</span>] atone for all the transgressions
    that arise from the <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span>; and the
    primary [vehicle of] atonement [<em>kaparah</em>] is through
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span> &mdash; for one who has sinned must bring from
    his <span class="key-term">mammon</span> and his money a
    <em>korban</em> [offering] commensurate with the sin. And through this the
    <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> atones for him, because in the Temple
    [<em>Beis ha-Mikdash</em>] there is the primary
    <span class="key-term">bitul</span>, and there the light of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above illuminates well &mdash; for
    the reception of the radiance of the light of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above is according to the holiness
    of the person, the place, and the time. Therefore the slave who is the monetary
    acquisition [<em>kinyan kaspo</em>] of a
    <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> can eat of
    <span class="key-term">terumah</span> and <em>kodashim</em>, for the primary
    perfection of the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> is that he rectifies
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span> from the
    <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span>, until all the objects of the
    world &mdash; which are all encompassed within
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span> &mdash; can receive the light of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above. And this is the primary
    rectification, as above.
  </p>

  <!-- SECTION 11 -->
  <span class="section-number">11</span>
  <p class="no-indent">
    And this is [the meaning of]:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;And you shall love Hashem your G-d with all your
    heart, and with all your soul, and with all your
    <em>me'odecha</em>&rdquo;</span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Deuteronomy 6:5]</span> &mdash;
    which is placed immediately following the verse:
    <em>&ldquo;Shema Yisrael &mdash; Hashem is our G-d, Hashem is One&rdquo;</em>
    &mdash; which is the primary aspect of
    <span class="key-term">bitul</span>: to nullify oneself and become included in
    His Oneness with <span class="key-term">mesiras nefesh</span>
    [total self-devotion] for the sanctification of the [Divine] Name, as our Sages
    of blessed memory stated. And afterwards one says:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;And you shall love Hashem your G-d with all your
    heart, and with all your soul, and with all your <em>me'odecha</em>&rdquo;</span>
    &mdash; which refers to <span class="key-term">mammon</span>, as is known &mdash;
    meaning to bind the light of the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> within
    one's heart, one's soul, and one's <em>me'odecha</em>, which is one's
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span>, wherein is encompassed all the desire of
    this world &mdash; [meaning] to nullify everything for the love of Hashem,
    blessed-be-He. For the primary binding of the light of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> is when one also purifies and sanctifies
    one's <span class="key-term">mammon</span> from the
    <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span>, wherein is the primary grip of
    the <span class="aramaic">zuhamas ha-nachash</span>, as above. And there [in
    <em>mammon</em>] is the primary rectification of the vessels &mdash; for the
    primary formation of the aspect of the vessels comes through the aspect of
    <em>smol, gevuros, tzimtzumim</em> [left side, severities, contractions] as is
    known &mdash; and this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mammon</span>,
    the aspect of <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;and in her left hand,
    wealth&rdquo;</span> etc.
    <span class="verse-source">[Proverbs 3:16, regarding the Torah]</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of the sanctity of Shabbos, which is the
    <span class="key-term">tachlis</span> [ultimate purpose], the aspect of the
    World to Come &mdash; for then all the thirty-nine
    <span class="key-term">melachos</span> are nullified. And then is the primary
    perfection of <span class="key-term">da'as</span> [knowledge/mind] to know Him,
    blessed-be-He, through the light of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above &mdash; because then all
    thirty-nine <span class="key-term">melachos</span> that are [rooted] in
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span> are nullified, as above. And therefore
    <span class="key-term">milah</span> [performed at its proper time] overrides
    Shabbos &mdash; for it is impossible to receive the sanctity of Shabbos, which is
    the <span class="key-term">tachlis</span>, except by first removing the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span> etc., as above.
  </p>

  <!-- SECTION 12 -->
  <span class="section-number">12</span>
  <p class="no-indent">
    And this is [the meaning of]:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;And it shall come to pass, because
    [<em>ekev</em>] you hearken to these statutes [<em>ha-mishpatim</em>]&rdquo;</span>
    etc. <span class="verse-source">[Deuteronomy 7:12]</span>. For the entire book of
    <em>Mishneh Torah</em> [Deuteronomy] is [a record of how] Moses admonishes Israel
    to fulfill the Torah and bind it well within their hearts so that it should not be
    forgotten and not pass from them &mdash; that is, the aspect mentioned above: that
    they should bind the light of the radiance of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above, from which all the Torah
    [flows], and bind it well within their hearts through engaging in Torah and
    <em>mitzvos</em>. And therefore he repeats and reviews all the Divine sparklings of
    Divinity that they had witnessed at the time of the Giving of the Torah and at the
    time of the Splitting of the Sea etc., as is explained in the preceding books [of
    the Torah] &mdash; as it is written in [the portion of]
    <em>Va'eschanan</em>:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;You have been shown to know that Hashem is the
    G-d&rdquo;</span> etc.
    <span class="verse-source">[Deuteronomy 4:35]</span>. And similarly, he there
    repeats and reviews all the great and awesome deeds of Hashem that He performed at
    the Giving of the Torah, when He spoke with them face to face etc. And afterwards
    [Moses] declared: <em>&ldquo;Shema Yisrael &mdash; Hashem is our G-d, Hashem is
    One&rdquo;</em> &mdash; which is the <em>mitzvah</em> of
    <span class="key-term">Kriyas Shema</span> every day, that every single member of
    Israel is obligated to recite <span class="key-term">Kriyas Shema</span> every day
    with <span class="key-term">mesiras nefesh</span> [total self-devotion] in order
    to be included in the One. And therefore, during
    <span class="key-term">Kriyas Shema</span>, one closes one's eyes &mdash; in order
    to be included in the <span class="key-term">tachlis</span>, which is entirely One;
    and it is impossible to become included there except through shutting one's eyes
    well, as above. And afterwards [Moses] declared:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;And you shall love&rdquo;</span> etc., as above
    &mdash; <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;and you shall bind them as a sign upon
    your arm&rdquo;</span> etc. &mdash; and all the subsequent portions, in all of
    which he admonishes them to fulfill the Torah, in order to bind the sparkling of
    His blessed Divinity within their hearts, as above. And connected to all of this
    is: <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;And it shall come to pass, because
    [<em>ekev</em>] you hearken&rdquo;</span> etc. &mdash; the word
    <span class="key-term">ekev</span> [literally: heel] refers to the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">ragley</span> [feet/heels], meaning: when you shall have
    bound the Torah within your hearts to such a degree that you hear it [reverberating]
    within the heels and feet &mdash; which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span>, which is called
    <span class="key-term">regel</span> [foot], as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;and all the substance that was at their feet
    [<em>b'ragleyhem</em>]&rdquo;</span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Genesis 7:3; and cf. Rashi on Numbers 22:22]</span>;
    and our Sages of blessed memory expounded: this refers to a person's
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span> that keeps him standing on his feet &mdash;
    then [will follow]:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;and Hashem your G-d will guard for you the
    covenant and the kindness&rdquo;</span> etc. For then you will have vessels to
    receive the <span class="key-term">chessed</span> [lovingkindness], as above.
    For the primary rectification of the vessels is accomplished when one rectifies the
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span>, wherein is the totality of all the desires
    of the <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span>, as above.
  </p>
  <p>
    And therefore [Moses] called the Torah here
    <span class="key-term">mishpatim</span> [literally: judgments / laws], as it is
    written: <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;And it shall come to pass, because you
    hearken to these <em>ha-mishpatim</em>&rdquo;</span> etc. For
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is a term also connoting
    <span class="key-term">yisoorim</span> [sufferings/afflictions], as Rashi explained
    in Isaiah, that <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> serves in three different
    usages etc. For the primary reception of the Torah comes through
    <span class="key-term">yisoorim</span>, through which one closes one's eyes from
    the <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span> and becomes included in the One
    &mdash; and through which one receives from there the
    <span class="key-term">mishpatim</span> of the Torah; and therefore the Torah is
    called <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>.
  </p>

  <!-- SECTION 13 -->
  <span class="section-number">13</span>
  <p class="no-indent">
    And this is what our Sages of blessed memory stated: that three things come
    through <span class="key-term">yisoorim</span> [sufferings], and they are: Torah,
    the Land of Israel [<span class="key-term">Eretz Yisrael</span>], and the World
    to Come [<span class="key-term">Olam ha-Ba</span>].
    <span class="verse-source">[Berachos 5a]</span>
    For all is one: for the World to Come is the
    <span class="key-term">tachlis</span> &mdash; when there will be fulfilled:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;On that day Hashem will be One and His Name will
    be One&rdquo;</span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Zechariah 14:9]</span> &mdash; that all will be
    entirely good, entirely One, as above; and it is impossible to merit this except
    through <span class="key-term">yisoorim</span>, through which one closes one's eyes
    from the <span class="aramaic">cheyzu d'hai alma</span>, as above. And this is also
    the aspect of Torah &mdash; for from there comes the entire reception of the Torah:
    from the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of the
    <span class="key-term">bitul</span> mentioned above, which comes through the
    <span class="key-term">yisoorim</span>, as above. And this is also the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisrael</span> [the Land of Israel] &mdash; for in
    the Land of Israel is the primary sanctified place wherein one merits to properly
    nullify oneself and draw from there the light of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above and bind it within one's
    heart properly. For in order to receive the Torah from the radiance of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above and bind it within one's
    heart properly &mdash; for this, one needs to sanctify the body through Torah and
    <em>mitzvos</em>; and according to the holiness of the body, so does one merit to
    bind [the <em>reshimu</em>] within one's heart, as above. And similarly, one needs
    the holiness of place, which is
    <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisrael</span> &mdash; and therefore one who dwells
    in the Land of Israel is likened to one who has a G-d
    <span class="verse-source">[Kesuvos 110b]</span>, for the primary sparkling of His
    blessed Divinity that is drawn from the radiance of the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above is [found] in the Land of
    Israel. And therefore, the entire book of <em>Devarim</em> [Deuteronomy] speaks
    extensively in praise of the Land of Israel &mdash; for the primary fulfillment of
    the Torah that is drawn from the radiance mentioned above is in the Land of Israel
    &mdash; and therefore one must endure <span class="key-term">yisoorim</span> before
    arriving in the Land of Israel, for the
    <span class="key-term">bitul</span> mentioned above, from which the
    <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> mentioned above shines, comes through
    <span class="key-term">yisoorim</span>, as above.
  </p>

  <!-- SECTION 14 -->
  <span class="section-number">14</span>
  <p class="no-indent">
    And this is [the meaning of]:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;And Hashem shall guard for you the covenant and
    the kindness [<em>chessed</em>] that He swore to your forefathers&rdquo;</span>
    &mdash; <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;shall guard&rdquo;</span>
    <span class="daikah">precisely</span> [<em>dayka</em>] &mdash; meaning that
    Hashem blessed-be-He will guard the <span class="key-term">chessed</span> so that
    it flows upon us in a manner in which we can receive it, so that the excess of
    <span class="key-term">chessed</span> should not harm us, G-d protect us, as above.
    And therefore, even though He has already sworn to our forefathers to do
    <span class="key-term">chessed</span> with us, nonetheless it is impossible to
    receive it except by fulfilling:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;And it shall come to pass, because
    [<em>ekev</em>] you hearken&rdquo;</span> etc., as above &mdash; that we hear all
    the <span class="key-term">mishpatim</span> of the Torah within the
    <span class="key-term">ragley</span> [feet] &mdash; in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;my foot stands on level ground&rdquo;</span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Psalms 26:12]</span> &mdash; meaning within the
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span> that is called
    <span class="key-term">ragley</span> &mdash; meaning that we should be firm in
    fulfilling the Torah and <em>mitzvos</em> to such a degree that
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span> will not cause us to transgress the Torah,
    G-d protect us. For in the desire for
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span> lies the primary danger lest one forget
    Hashem blessed-be-He through an abundance of money, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;and silver and gold will multiply for you and
    your heart will become haughty and you will forget Hashem&rdquo;</span> etc.
    <span class="verse-source">[Deuteronomy 8:13&ndash;14]</span>; and as it is
    written [in the beginning of <em>Devarim</em>]:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;<em>v'di zahav</em>&rdquo;</span>
    [and abundant gold] &mdash; as our Sages of blessed memory expounded there
    <span class="verse-source">[Deuteronomy 1:1, Berachos 32a]</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    But if you merit to hear the <span class="key-term">mishpatim</span> of the Torah
    even in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">akavim</span> [heels] and
    <span class="key-term">ragley</span> [feet] &mdash; meaning within the
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span>, as above &mdash; then:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;And Hashem shall guard for you the
    <em>chessed</em>&rdquo;</span> &mdash; that no foreign [force] shall rule over it
    &mdash; so that the abundance of <span class="key-term">chessed</span> should not
    harm you at all, G-d protect us. For <em>&ldquo;one who comes to purify himself
    is aided&rdquo;</em>
    <span class="verse-source">[Shabbos 104a]</span>. And since you strengthen yourself
    to remember Hashem blessed-be-He even within [the domain of]
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span>, Hashem blessed-be-He will also guard the
    <span class="key-term">chessed</span> for you &mdash; meaning He will pour upon you
    the flow of <span class="key-term">chessed</span> with great watchfulness, in a
    manner that will not harm you at all &mdash; in the aspect of:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;May Hashem bless you&rdquo;</span> &mdash; with
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span>;
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;and guard you&rdquo;</span> &mdash; from the
    destructive forces [<span class="aramaic">mazeekay alma</span>], so that the
    destructive forces of the world should not gain hold of you through an abundance of
    <span class="key-term">mammon</span>, G-d protect us
    <span class="verse-source">[Numbers 6:24 &mdash; the Priestly Blessing, as expounded
    by our Sages]</span> &mdash; and then
    <span class="daikah">precisely</span>:
    <span class="verse-quote">&ldquo;and He will love you and bless you and multiply
    you, and bless the fruit of your womb and the fruit of your land&rdquo;</span> etc.
    <span class="verse-source">[Deuteronomy 7:13]</span> &mdash; for He will pour upon
    you all goodness, since you will be capable of receiving the abundant flow of
    <span class="key-term">chessed</span> through the rectification of the vessels,
    as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY -->
  <div class="summary-box">
    <h3>🌿 Translator's Summary &mdash; <em>Likutay Halachos</em>, Yoreh Dayah, Laws of <em>Milah</em> 5 (continued) &mdash; <em>Avadim</em></h3>

    <p class="summary-para">
      Picking up immediately after section&nbsp;8 (&sect;&nbsp;ח) of Milah&nbsp;5, the author bridges
      to the <em>Shulchan Aruch</em>'s laws of <strong>circumcision of slaves</strong> (SA YD
      Hilchos Milah 5:9). This is a characteristic move in <em>Likutay Halachos</em>: the same
      kabbalistic framework that illuminated one halacha is now extended to shed light on an adjacent
      one, revealing the inner unity of the entire <em>Shulchan Aruch</em>.
    </p>

    <p class="summary-para">
      <strong>The slave as monetary extension:</strong> The master is obligated to circumcise his
      slave because the slave is his <em>kinyan kaspo</em> (monetary acquisition). This halacha
      becomes a gateway to the deepest teaching of the section: the
      <span style="font-style:italic;">zuhamas ha-nachash</span> (spiritual filth of the serpent)
      grips most intensely not in the body generally, but specifically in the domain of
      <em>mammon</em> (money/wealth). Within <em>mammon</em> reside all thirty-nine
      <em>melachos</em> (creative labors) that flowed from Adam's sin &mdash; and these
      thirty-nine <em>melachos</em>, driven by financial desire, are the precise opposite of
      <em>bitul</em>. Therefore, no rectification of vessels is complete without purifying this
      monetary dimension &mdash; represented by the slave.
    </p>

    <p class="summary-para">
      <strong>The <em>Kohen</em>'s slave and <em>terumah</em>:</strong> The rule that a
      <em>Kohen</em>'s slave may eat <em>terumah</em> and <em>kodashim</em> (which even ordinary
      Israelites may not) is decoded: the <em>Kohen</em>'s entire Temple service is the
      rectification of the thirty-nine <em>melachos</em> &mdash; i.e., the domain of
      <em>mammon</em>. Since the slave is the <em>Kohen</em>'s monetary extension
      (<em>kaspo</em>), and the <em>Kohen</em> has achieved vessel-rectification at this level,
      the slave shares in the capacity to receive holy food without harm.
    </p>

    <p class="summary-para">
      <strong><em>Shema</em> and <em>me'odecha</em>:</strong> The word <em>me'odecha</em>
      ("with all your very much / all your resources") is understood as <em>mammon</em>. The
      sequence <em>Shema</em> &rarr; <em>bitul</em> &rarr; <em>V'ahavta / me'odecha</em>
      maps the process: first nullify into the One, then bind the <em>reshimu</em> into one's
      heart and soul &mdash; and finally into one's financial domain. This is the last and most
      difficult frontier of rectification.
    </p>

    <p class="summary-para">
      <strong>Torah, <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>, and <em>Olam ha-Ba</em> through suffering:</strong>
      These three (Berachos 5a) all come through <em>yisoorim</em> because suffering forces
      the closing of eyes from <em>cheyzu d'hai alma</em> &mdash; producing the
      <em>bitul</em> from which the <em>reshimu</em> shines. All three are in truth one
      single process of inclusion in the <em>tachlis</em>.
    </p>

    <p class="summary-para">
      <strong>"And Hashem will guard for you the <em>chessed</em>"</strong> &mdash; the word
      "guard" (<em>v'shamar</em>) is read <em>dayka</em> (precisely/specifically): He will
      guard the <em>chessed</em> so it does not overwhelm unready vessels. When a person
      succeeds in remembering Hashem even within <em>mammon</em> (the <em>ekev</em>/heels),
      Hashem reciprocates by pouring <em>chessed</em> in a guarded manner that will not harm
      &mdash; culminating in the Priestly Blessing: "May Hashem bless you [with
      <em>mammon</em>] and guard you [from the destructive forces that cling to <em>mammon</em>]."
    </p>

